  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 AM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nA mid-level ridge located over the central Great Plains continues to \nbe the predominant feature driving hot and relatively dry conditions \nacross the CWA on Wednesday into Thursday morning. Subsidence aloft \nwill promote highs in the upper-90s along the Coastal Bend, and up \nto 100-105 degF elsewhere. These highs coupled with dewpoints in the \n60s and 70s will promote heat indices ranging from 105-113 degF, \nintroducing a moderate to major (levels 2/3-4) HeatRisk. A Heat \nAdvisory is in effect Wednesday throughout much of the CWA. The \nforecast changes very little on Thursday, so an additional Heat \nAdvisory is likely.\n\nTropical Storm Bertha continues to move slowly westward across the \nnorthern Gulf. The cyclone remains heavily sheared, and the low \nlevel center is exposed. Bertha is now expected to gradually weaken \nas vertical shear increases and land interaction becomes likely. The \nstorm is forecast to pass over or just south of Coastal Louisiana \nand arrive in the vicinity of East Texas on Thursday. The \naforementioned vertical shear will accentuate the northeast to \nsouthwest tilt of TS Bertha's vortex. As a consequence, PVA and deep \nmoisture associated with TS Bertha may be in place over the CWA on \nThursday evening into the early overnight hours. Due to this \nenvironment, isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms (35-\n55%) are possible. While TS Bertha will likely remain north and lose \nstrength, higher surf and an elevated risk of rip currents is \ngenerally expected this weekend.",
